WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.550
SD-WAN is the governor's first layer.

2
00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:08.032
It splits traffic into a lean class, consensus,
voice and sign-in, and a bulk class,

3
00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:10.056
backups, patches and re-syncs,

4
00:00:10.056 --> 00:00:15.200
and keeps bulk transfers from queuing
ahead of latency-critical packets.

5
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.951
It recovers route inflation:
Microsoft measured a median 78

6
00:00:19.951 --> 00:00:25.462
milliseconds from UK South to East
US, against a fiber floor of about 62.

7
00:00:25.462 --> 00:00:28.919
And it tightens the tail with
forward error correction,

8
00:00:28.919 --> 00:00:31.324
packet duplication and fast failover.

9
00:00:31.324 --> 00:00:34.238
It reaches the floor;
it never goes below it.

10
00:00:34.238 --> 00:00:37.472
SASE sits above it: secure web gateway,

11
00:00:37.472 --> 00:00:41.212
CASB, firewall and zero
trust network access.

12
00:00:41.212 --> 00:00:46.247
CISA lists SD-WAN among SASE's
networking tools, but mandates neither,

13
00:00:46.247 --> 00:00:51.112
and SD-WAN alone doesn't close the
boundary, flow and malware controls.

14
00:00:51.112 --> 00:00:52.275
Two cautions.

15
00:00:52.275 --> 00:00:54.480
The enforcement point has a location,

16
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.250
so a distant point of presence is
a hairpin with a different name.

17
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.387
And FedRAMP status differs
by offering, not by vendor,

18
00:01:01.387 --> 00:01:04.800
so read the Marketplace when
the requirement is written.
